Figure 1c shows the time-dependent MTS viability in order to determine the kinetics of toxicity of the three MOFs on MCF-7 cells both as a function of DDS and DCA concentration. For the two materials containing TPP, we observe toxicity as early as \(4~\mathrm{h}\). At DDS concentration of \(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\), viability decreases to \(78\%\) and \(66\%\) for \(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-TPP\(_{5}\)-UiO-66 and TPP@(\(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66), respectively, decreasing further as time progresses but stabilizing between 8 and \(48~\mathrm{h}\) down to values of ca. \(60 - 50\%\). At \(72~\mathrm{h}\), viability reduces further down to 36 and \(12\%\) for both MOFs at \(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) concentration. \(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66, on the other hand, remains non-toxic up to \(48~\mathrm{h}\), showing only slight toxicity after \(72~\mathrm{h}\) at the highest concentration of \(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (viability ca. \(63\%\)). When plotted as a function of DCA concentration, it is clear that TPP@(\(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66), which has the lowest levels of DCA loading and TPP present only on the external surface, is the most toxic material, causing a sharp decrease in cell viability after as soon as \(4~\mathrm{h}\) of incubation. Interestingly, when comparing the toxicity of \(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-TPP\(_{5}\)-UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66 based on DCA concentration, they show similar performance for times below \(72~\mathrm{h}\). At \(72~\mathrm{h}\), \(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-TPP\(_{5}\)-UiO-66 seems to be more active than \(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66. Whereas it is clear that the addition of TPP to the external surface dramatically increases the efficacy of the UiO-66-based DDS, its addition during the UiO-66 synthesis does not show the same benefits. This is probably because TPP loading is much lower, and it may be integrated throughout the MOF matrix and therefore cannot target the particles to the mitochondria – showing only a beneficial effect at \(72~\mathrm{h}\), perhaps due to the partial degradation and release of TPP to the media. This observation of the TPP located on the external surface area was confirmed by XPS, as described above.

To confirm that TPP is directing the DDS to the mitochondria, we first tracked the particles in vitro, imaging them with a modified fluorescent pyrene group on the TPP (fTPP), creating fTPP@(\(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66). Figure 2a shows the confocal images of MCF-7 cells after \(2~\mathrm{h}\) of incubation with fTPP@(\(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66). Mitochondria are represented in red, and nuclei in blue. In the merged images, we observe some co-localization between fTPP@(\(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66) and RFP-labelled mitochondria, as illustrated by the yellow color (shown by white arrows). Additionally, most of the non-overlapping signal originating from fTPP@(\(\mathrm{DCA}_{5}\)-UiO-66) is heavily concentrated around the mitochondria. This suggests that the DDS is being targeted without necessarily being completely taken up by mitochondria, possibly due to the size of the DDS being too large for mitochondrial uptake. Given the observed cytotoxic effect, mitochondrial uptake might not be necessary to observe an increase in the efficacy of DCA. The mere accumulation of particles near the mitochondria may cause high local concentrations of toxic drug.
